.TH OCTOVAULT 1 "October 2026" "OctoVault 1.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
octovault \- manage encrypted local vaults and independent backups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B octovault
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --system ]
.I GROUP ACTION
.RI [ --option " VALUE" ...]
.br
.B octovault
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --system ]
.B request
.RI [ FILE | - ]
.br
.B octovault
.RB [ --json ]
.B job
.BR get | cancel
.I ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
OctoVault provides a terminal client for the same authorized service used by
its native GNOME application. Run as your regular account. The client starts
authorization when a protected operation requires it, using approved personal
access when available or administrator authentication through PolicyKit.
Running with sudo selects the root account's configuration. An administrator
approving a request does not become the owner of the caller's data.
.SH GROUPS
.TP
.B access
status, unlock, lock, list, users, grant, and revoke. Use --user LOGIN to
select the local account for grant or revoke. Changing another account's
personal access requires fresh administrator approval.
.TP
.B vault
list, get, create, import, open, close, update, remove, resize, passwd,
setkey, key-export, index, search, and auth-check.
.TP
.B migration
add, list, and recover.
.TP
.B destination
list, get, save, test, and remove.
.TP
.B repository
list, create, import, recovery, check, stats, snapshots, files, find, diff,
restore, forget, prune, password, copy, and remove.
.TP
.B backup
list, save, remove, run, and history.
.TP
.B schedule
sync.
.TP
.B diagnostic
list and get. Private failure summaries are scoped to your account.
.SH AUTHORIZATION
Saved metadata and diagnostic reports can be inspected without management
authorization. Repository data access, record changes, and vault operations
require authorization even when an operation does not modify data.
.PP
A reusable authorization session belongs to the caller UID, its unique bus
connection, and its actual active, local, unlocked login session. It lasts
at most eight hours. Explicit lock, connection loss, desktop lock, logout,
suspension, loss of the active session, service restart, policy change,
revocation, or expiry ends it. Ending authorization does not close an open
vault or cancel work already running.
.PP
Each CLI invocation has its own bus connection. A standalone access unlock
command exits and does not authorize subsequent commands or the desktop.
Normally run the required operation and let the CLI authorize that connection.
Supported headless or remote operations require fresh administrator approval
per operation; they do not create a reusable local-session authorization.
The CLI starts a PolicyKit terminal agent when needed.
.PP
An administrator can enroll a local account for personal access without
adding it to sudo. Personal backup and restore processes run as the owner,
so filesystem permissions still restrict their sources, repositories, and
restore targets. Personal access cannot operate system-scope records or
request forced vault closure. Older records without execution_scope remain
system-scope; grants do not silently convert them. Administrator management
of a personal record does not change the record's scope.
.PP
Saved schedules do not require an open management session. Personal schedules
require a valid personal grant when they start. Revocation stops future
personal scheduled backups; running jobs finish safely, and saved vault
safety-close policies remain in effect. Vault passphrases and YubiKey
PIN or touch requirements remain independent of management authorization.
.SH OPTIONS
Use --id to select an existing record. Option names use hyphens. Repeat
--source, --exclude, --include, or --close-on to supply multiple array entries.
Boolean options accept true or false, a bare flag, or a --no-flag form.
--size-gb is capacity in decimal gigabytes.
.PP
For vault create, --directory selects the folder holding the new container;
--name determines its filename. Advanced callers may supply --file instead.
A new encrypted key is generated and kept in the account's private managed key
directory unless --use-existing-key selects an existing --key-file.
For a new image key, --image-source supplies a cover image whose original is
preserved. Text keys are generated automatically. --auth gpg supports software
OpenPGP keys and compatible tokens; a new key requires --recipient.
Add --require-token to require the matching card-backed encryption key.
The auth-check action verifies the recipient and PIN or passphrase before
creating any vault. Creation performs this check automatically.
The unlock folder is managed automatically. Key paths are remembered;
vault passphrases are never saved.
.PP
Creation makes an empty container and does not move existing folders.
After opening it, use migration add to protect a selected original path;
--source is the existing file or folder and --relative is its name inside
the encrypted filesystem. OctoVault creates its folder mapping automatically.
After successful protection, opening the vault shows the protected contents
at the original path. Closing reveals a separate ordinary underlying folder
or file. Content written there while locked is not encrypted or merged.
.PP
A retained .octovault-staging-* location is an original copy from unfinished
folder protection, not the encrypted container. Preserve it and inspect
migration list before choosing migration recover with --action resume or,
when the recorded stage permits it, --action rollback. Recovery refuses
to overwrite an occupied original path. The --migration-id value comes
from the migration_id field of the recovery record.
.PP
Vault protection does not create a versioned backup. Preserve the encrypted
container and its separate encrypted key or carrier, plus the authentication
material needed to unlock it, in an independent recovery arrangement.
.PP
Secret options such as --password, --old-password, --secret-key,
--ssh-private-key, and --ssh-passphrase take no argument and prompt on the
terminal without echo. Never place secrets in command-line arguments.
Structured requests may supply secrets through standard input. Do not retain
secret requests in shell history or unprotected files.
.TP
.B --json
Emit machine-readable results.
.TP
.B --system
Request administrator authorization instead of automatically selecting
approved personal access. Use this for administrator-managed resources.
.TP
.B --help
Show command usage.
.TP
.B --version
Show the application version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /var/lib/octovault/access.json
Root-owned personal access policy, mode 0600. Grants match local account
names and numeric user IDs. Manage grants through access grant or access revoke.
.TP
.B /var/lib/octovault/users/UID
Root-owned account state and encrypted credentials. Retained on package removal.
.TP
.B /var/lib/octovault/users/UID/diagnostics
The most recent 100 private failure summaries. Read them with diagnostic list
or diagnostic get --id ID, or through Activity in the desktop application.
.TP
.B /usr/libexec/octovault
Fixed privileged workers; not a public invocation interface.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Failed, rejected, or cancelled operations return nonzero.
.SH RECOVERY
Save repository addresses, remote credentials, and repository passwords outside
the computer. Host-bound encrypted credentials alone are not portable recovery
information. The explicit repository recovery action requires fresh owner
authentication when using personal access to a personal repository, or fresh
administrator authentication when using management access. Personal restore cannot preserve arbitrary
system ownership or write paths the owner cannot access.
